<Guide
  path="/guide/"
  title="What a completed repair means"
  description="A repair is complete when the record says what was done, what was checked and by whom, whether the resident's problem is solved, and who closed it and why."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">The guide</p>
  <h1>What "completed" means</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A job marked "done" can mean many things: the contractor left, the work was tested, the resident is happy, or
    somebody closed the ticket to tidy the list. A good record says which.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most repair systems have a status field, and when the contractor reports the work as done, that field turns
    green. The trouble starts later. The resident writes that the leak is back. The owner asks what was actually
    replaced. Another contractor comes and finds the same fault. Nobody can tell from the record what was claimed,
    what was checked, and what the resident ever said.
  </p>

  <p>Open Repair Record answers one question for every closed job:</p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>The contractor closed the job. What exactly was done, what was checked, and is the resident's problem solved?</strong></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who says what</h2>
  <p>
    The core idea is to keep each source of information separate. A contractor's statement, a coordinator's check
    and a resident's answer are different things, with different weight. A record that merges them into one "done"
    loses exactly the information people need when something goes wrong.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The contractor</strong> says what they did and what they believe the outcome is.</li>
    <li><strong>A check</strong> says who looked, how (a test run, a measurement, a photo), what they found, and what they did not cover.</li>
    <li><strong>The resident</strong> says whether the problem they reported is solved, in their view. Or they say nothing, and the record says that too.</li>
    <li><strong>The coordinator</strong> decides to close the record, and says why.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A quick test for any completion report</h2>
  <p>Before you close a job, the report should let a stranger answer these questions:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What was reported, where, and when?</li>
    <li>Who says the work is done, and what exactly did they do?</li>
    <li>Was anything checked? By whom, how, and what did the check not cover?</li>
    <li>What did the resident say? If nothing, were they asked, and when?</li>
    <li>Is anything left to do? Who owns it, and by when?</li>
    <li>Who closed the record, and for what reason?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If any answer is "I don't know", the report is not wrong, it is just incomplete. The
    <a href="/build/">builder</a> shows you which parts are missing before you send it, and the
    <a href="/template/">printable template</a> asks the same questions on paper.
  </p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <p>
    A repair record documents claims and checks. It is not a safety certificate, an inspection, or a legal sign-off,
    and it does not replace any inspection or certificate your local rules require. A resident's "it's fixed"
    confirms what they see; it says nothing about the safety of the equipment. A photo shows what it shows. The
    record is useful precisely because it is honest about these differences.
  </p>
</Guide>
